00ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;fQaivIrDD1J_dMnBDKSWKBorC3N4SFtsDCD0-tlQSt4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarks0.32022839999999997ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;zB-bqCnsRFWvYHoLpe5qhQAa4Qm81yDbQ876T89bG48ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka2ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;YtPv1IiK2V9wI8xqzsSYD6ZkNzica3wad-CVE_BJAPoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke32000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;mxp1IDKBrjIu6bi0sTWpCGlJEXTfm4IwYF9vpRQOn68ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkd6928ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;-aEu0pJxA6NmGzfBUH-ibb3Mhwqb0wBK6Fk-7H4szqYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;rfZaRlMMaA_fZ7mSICUn0OTO_KDaohr9nK7IlOH_Rk4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka2ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;0d60_Icr1MJYBfRwPJXCC16XDw32flWZg9z46QnuxYgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;X1WZ_xHGy2ob64iBTsvR_aoPSW-QDxecV_SxoSMIjosƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyæ{"tool":"submit_revision_request","failureLayer":"writer-model","replayFrom":"Revise the existing draft body, lede, summary, and description â€” do not replay from sources.","summary":"The draft is structurally sound and draws well on the admitted sources, but three bounded defects block publication: a misattribution in the lede (and summary/description) that credits the Geometry article with a claim it never makes; a strengthened paraphrase of the MbappÃ© quote that adds \"primary reason\" without warrant; and forward-looking \"will\" constructions in speculative interpretive sentences that violate the writing law. All repairs are within the writer-model layer.","requiredRepairs":["MISATTRIBUTION IN LEDE, SUMMARY, AND DESCRIPTION: The phrase 'clash between the world's two highest-ranked nations' is attributed via inline hyperlink to [Geometry, football and the World Cup final](https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792). The Geometry source text does not contain that language. The claim â€” 'a fitting finale between the world's two highest-ranked teams' â€” appears only in ['Tactically complete' Spain vs an inspired Messi-led Argentina](https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372). The attribution must be corrected in the lede, the summary front-matter field, and the description front-matter field so the link and attribution point to the source that actually carries the claim.","STRENGTHENED ATTRIBUTION ON MBAPPÃ‰ QUOTE: The draft states MbappÃ© cited the lack of communication 'as the primary reason for his team's inability to organize.' The Geometry source reports only that MbappÃ© said there had been 'a lack of communication' in France's pressing â€” no primacy claim is made. Remove 'primary reason' and render the attribution as the source warrants, e.g. 'cited a lack of communication in France's pressing.'","BARE 'WILL' IN SPECULATIVE FORWARD-LOOKING SENTENCES: The writing law prohibits 'will' outside quoted source titles for pre-event interpretation. The final body paragraph contains 'will determine the final outcome of the 2026 World Cup' and the body elsewhere uses 'will likely attempt' in interpretive, not factual-scheduling, contexts. Replace with hedged modality language ('may', 'could', 'might') in all speculative or analytical forward-looking constructions. (The factual scheduling claim 'Spain and Argentina will meet at MetLife Stadium' â€” drawn verbatim from the source â€” may be rendered with the source's own language or lightly hedged, but all writer-generated interpretive 'will' clauses must be corrected.)"],"report":"\n## Editorial Assessment â€” geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final\n\n### Deterministic Standards\nThe deterministic gate reads `publishable: true` / `draft: true`. The single warning (`attention-preserve-modality`) flags certainty language in the description/summary surface. That warning is directly traceable to the misattribution identified below, not to an independent editorial failure. The `gates_clear: false` flag is a pipeline state; this review does not request it be toggled.\n\n### Headline and Lede Faithfulness\nThe headline \"Spain's Geometry Meets Messi's Magic in World Cup Final Showdown\" is a metaphorical editorial frame for an analytical article about the upcoming final's tactical contrast. \"Meets\" refers to the scheduled matchup and the structural contrast between the two sides' approaches, not to a predicted outcome â€” it is defensible framing. The lede, however, contains a material attribution error (see below) that contaminates the summary and description fields as well.\n\n### Material Claim Audit\n\n**Lede / Summary / Description â€” MISATTRIBUTION**\nThe phrase \"clash between the world's two highest-ranked nations\" is linked to and attributed to [Geometry, football and the World Cup final](https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792). Reading that source in full, the claim does not appear. The Geometry article opens by establishing the match at MetLife Stadium and proceeds directly to tactical analysis; it never characterises the participants by FIFA ranking. The ranking claim â€” \"a fitting finale between the world's two highest-ranked teams\" â€” appears in ['Tactically complete' Spain vs an inspired Messi-led Argentina](https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372), a separate source. A reader following the inline hyperlink to verify the ranking claim would not find it there. This is a direct misattribution of a factual assertion and must be corrected in the lede body, the summary front-matter field, and the description front-matter field. The description field also appears to have been generated from the corrupted lede and ends in truncation (\"identifies asâ€¦\"), which is a visible artefact of the same error.\n\n**MbappÃ© Attribution â€” STRENGTHENED**\nThe draft writes: \"Kylian MbappÃ© cited a 'lack of communication' as the primary reason for his team's inability to organize against these sequences.\" The Geometry source reports: MbappÃ© \"said there had been 'a lack of communication' in France's pressing.\" The source establishes that he named this factor; it does not rank it as the primary or definitive reason. Adding \"primary reason\" upgrades reported attribution to editorial conclusion and must be removed.\n\n**Forward-Looking 'Will' Constructions â€” MODALITY VIOLATION**\nThe writing law is explicit: \"a vision is never a prophecy: write 'might' or 'could'. NEVER use the word 'will' outside a quoted source title.\" The final body paragraph contains \"will determine the final outcome of the 2026 World Cup,\" a speculative interpretive sentence generated by the writer model. Elsewhere \"Rodri, will likely attempt to maintain the team's technical tempo\" uses \"will\" in an analytical pre-match prediction. These must be rendered with hedged modality language. (The factual scheduling statement that Spain and Argentina meet at MetLife Stadium is drawn from the sources and may follow the source's own phrasing, or be lightly hedged; the prohibition primarily governs writer-generated speculative language.)\n\n### Remaining Body Observations\n- The Chicago Bulls / Phil Jackson / Tex Winter triangle offence comparison is correctly sourced and attributed to the Geometry article. âœ“\n- Pedro Porro's 58th-minute goal with Dani Olmo is correctly sourced and attributed. âœ“\n- Spain conceding one goal; six players scoring; Mikel Oyarzabal leading with five; Rodri's midfield role; Unai SimÃ³n and Dani Olmo descriptions are all drawn from the 'Tactically complete' source and accurately rendered. âœ“\n- The Argentina-as-collective-sacrifice-for-Messi frame is supported by the 'Tactically complete' source and is presented as interpretation, not fact. âœ“\n- The passing-network research and player-tracking data references (PLOS ONE and Nature journal) are mentioned in the source and handled with appropriate indirection in the draft. âœ“\n- Sections build progressively; there is no section-by-section source mapping; prose is mostly specific and grounded. The final paragraph is the weakest â€” it drifts into abstract framing â€” but this is a modality and \"will\" problem addressable by the same repair noted above.\n\n### Source Coverage and Fairness\nNo disputed conduct, no named individual subjected to allegation, no right-of-reply requirement triggered. Both sources are academic analysis pieces; neither involves commercial interest (both authors disclose no relevant affiliations beyond academic appointments). No fairness defect identified.\n\n### Pipeline Diagnosis\nThe sources are sufficient, the assignment is coherent, and the evidence budget supports the article's scope. The three defects are drafting errors: a cross-source attribution failure, a quote-attribution strengthening, and modality violations in forward-looking prose. All are bounded repairs at the writer-model layer. No replay from sources is required.\n"}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;fPSZa0zqRY6yDNDCbwirH2-25Tog9XefUKEOuNGAeGYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$693d328b-334e-43d6-8ee9-22c221243fd2ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;abdMjjATy582zB3wk1I-UsZCs7Qgsfha4dn547zD-foƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhtool_useƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;TF7eCXHCuRvlTCaw8-Cz5LLpvQ6Vbm7npH--dqFHUrIƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;dq1-XNYCMWlDAt726u_tJm1f2e7u-QEbO29RQKIlMMEƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarklwriter-modelƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;bjB24Qr_8_rR7auY1yLvv7n5c9Ia6iOSbq51wE_5g0Qƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx^Revise the existing draft body, lede, summary, and description â€” do not replay from sources.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;sAJ6NJJCWpntY2lLD142dQuKyQhNWTD9NYQ_4aAIh3cƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky
## Editorial Assessment â€” geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final

### Deterministic Standards
The deterministic gate reads `publishable: true` / `draft: true`. The single warning (`attention-preserve-modality`) flags certainty language in the description/summary surface. That warning is directly traceable to the misattribution identified below, not to an independent editorial failure. The `gates_clear: false` flag is a pipeline state; this review does not request it be toggled.

### Headline and Lede Faithfulness
The headline "Spain's Geometry Meets Messi's Magic in World Cup Final Showdown" is a metaphorical editorial frame for an analytical article about the upcoming final's tactical contrast. "Meets" refers to the scheduled matchup and the structural contrast between the two sides' approaches, not to a predicted outcome â€” it is defensible framing. The lede, however, contains a material attribution error (see below) that contaminates the summary and description fields as well.

### Material Claim Audit

**Lede / Summary / Description â€” MISATTRIBUTION**
The phrase "clash between the world's two highest-ranked nations" is linked to and attributed to [Geometry, football and the World Cup final](https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792). Reading that source in full, the claim does not appear. The Geometry article opens by establishing the match at MetLife Stadium and proceeds directly to tactical analysis; it never characterises the participants by FIFA ranking. The ranking claim â€” "a fitting finale between the world's two highest-ranked teams" â€” appears in ['Tactically complete' Spain vs an inspired Messi-led Argentina](https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372), a separate source. A reader following the inline hyperlink to verify the ranking claim would not find it there. This is a direct misattribution of a factual assertion and must be corrected in the lede body, the summary front-matter field, and the description front-matter field. The description field also appears to have been generated from the corrupted lede and ends in truncation ("identifies asâ€¦"), which is a visible artefact of the same error.

**MbappÃ© Attribution â€” STRENGTHENED**
The draft writes: "Kylian MbappÃ© cited a 'lack of communication' as the primary reason for his team's inability to organize against these sequences." The Geometry source reports: MbappÃ© "said there had been 'a lack of communication' in France's pressing." The source establishes that he named this factor; it does not rank it as the primary or definitive reason. Adding "primary reason" upgrades reported attribution to editorial conclusion and must be removed.

**Forward-Looking 'Will' Constructions â€” MODALITY VIOLATION**
The writing law is explicit: "a vision is never a prophecy: write 'might' or 'could'. NEVER use the word 'will' outside a quoted source title." The final body paragraph contains "will determine the final outcome of the 2026 World Cup," a speculative interpretive sentence generated by the writer model. Elsewhere "Rodri, will likely attempt to maintain the team's technical tempo" uses "will" in an analytical pre-match prediction. These must be rendered with hedged modality language. (The factual scheduling statement that Spain and Argentina meet at MetLife Stadium is drawn from the sources and may follow the source's own phrasing, or be lightly hedged; the prohibition primarily governs writer-generated speculative language.)

### Remaining Body Observations
- The Chicago Bulls / Phil Jackson / Tex Winter triangle offence comparison is correctly sourced and attributed to the Geometry article. âœ“
- Pedro Porro's 58th-minute goal with Dani Olmo is correctly sourced and attributed. âœ“
- Spain conceding one goal; six players scoring; Mikel Oyarzabal leading with five; Rodri's midfield role; Unai SimÃ³n and Dani Olmo descriptions are all drawn from the 'Tactically complete' source and accurately rendered. âœ“
- The Argentina-as-collective-sacrifice-for-Messi frame is supported by the 'Tactically complete' source and is presented as interpretation, not fact. âœ“
- The passing-network research and player-tracking data references (PLOS ONE and Nature journal) are mentioned in the source and handled with appropriate indirection in the draft. âœ“
- Sections build progressively; there is no section-by-section source mapping; prose is mostly specific and grounded. The final paragraph is the weakest â€” it drifts into abstract framing â€” but this is a modality and "will" problem addressable by the same repair noted above.

### Source Coverage and Fairness
No disputed conduct, no named individual subjected to allegation, no right-of-reply requirement triggered. Both sources are academic analysis pieces; neither involves commercial interest (both authors disclose no relevant affiliations beyond academic appointments). No fairness defect identified.

### Pipeline Diagnosis
The sources are sufficient, the assignment is coherent, and the evidence budget supports the article's scope. The three defects are drafting errors: a cross-source attribution failure, a quote-attribution strengthening, and modality violations in forward-looking prose. All are bounded repairs at the writer-model layer. No replay from sources is required.
